.TH DAVID.TOML 5 "November 2026" "david-server 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
david.toml \- the settings of the machine for the David server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/david.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
The file holds what belongs to the machine: the account the server runs as, the rights on the
library folders, where it listens, the data folder, the certificate, extra groups and hidden features.
It is TOML. A missing file means the defaults; a key that the server does not know, or a value of the
wrong type, stops the start with a message that names the file and the place.
.PP
A value comes from, in this order: the command line, the environment
.RB ( DAVID_... ),
this file, the built\-in default. Everything the family decides in the administration pages (import
folders, rules, the CD settings, profiles and rights) lives in the database, not here.
.PP
Changes apply at the next start:
.BR "systemctl restart david" .
The package marks the file as configuration, so an update keeps it.
.B DAVID_CONFIG
names another file.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BR user " = " \(dq david \(dq
The account the server runs as. The service starts as root, gives this user the rights on the library
folders, hands it the data folder and drops to it for good.
.TP
.BR uid " = " \fIn\fP ", " gid " = " \fIn\fP
The numeric IDs of the account. The package creates the account with them; without them the system
picks its own. Set both before the first install if the IDs must be the same on every machine (a NAS
that maps them).
.TP
.BR acl " = " true | false
Give the user the rights on the audiobook and music folders at every start, with POSIX ACLs (like
.B setfacl \-R \-m u:UID:rwX
plus a default entry for new files). Needs the start as root. Default
.BR true .
.TP
.BR data_dir " = " \(dq\fIpath\fP\(dq
The folder of the database, the certificate and the staging area of the CD import. Default
.BR /var/lib/david .
Like
.B \-\-data\-dir
and
.BR DAVID_DATA_DIR .
.TP
.BR listen " = " \(dq\fIaddr\fP\(dq " | [" \(dq\fIaddr\fP\(dq ", ...]"
Where the interface listens: one address, or a list that is served at the same time. IPv4 and IPv6 are
separate addresses. Default
.BR 127.0.0.1:8484 ;
the packaged service sets
.B 0.0.0.0:8484
and
.B [::]:8484
in its unit. Without addresses that can speak TLS (loopback only) there is no HTTPS.
Like
.B \-\-listen
and
.B DAVID_LISTEN
(a list separated by commas there).
.TP
.BR mcp " = " true | false
Serve
.B /mcp
for a language model that looks after the tags. Default
.BR false .
Access still needs a token made in the settings. Like
.B \-\-mcp
and
.BR DAVID_MCP .
.TP
.BR log " = " \(dq\fIlevel\fP\(dq
.BR error ", " warn ", " info " (default), " debug " or " trace .
Also a setting in the pages, where it applies at once.
.TP
.BR tls_cert " = " \(dq\fIfile\fP\(dq ", " tls_key " = " \(dq\fIfile\fP\(dq
A certificate from a certificate authority (Let's Encrypt, ...) instead of the self\-made one, both in
PEM. The service user has to be able to read them (a group that may read the certificates, in
.BR groups ).
.TP
.BR tls_host " = " \(dq\fIname\fP\(dq
The name devices connect to with such a certificate: they check the name, not a fingerprint, and the
pairing code carries the name.
.TP
.BR tls_reload_s " = " \fIseconds\fP
How often a renewed certificate is looked for. Default 300. The certificate keys can also be set in the
pages (Settings, Certificate); the flags,
.B DAVID_TLS_*
and this file win.
.TP
.BR groups " = [" \(dq\fIgroup\fP\(dq ", ...]"
Groups the service is a member of in addition to those of its account, looked up by name when it starts
as root; a group that does not exist is skipped with a message. For example
.B ssl\-cert
to read the certificates, or
.B cdrom
to use the CD drive. The server sets no rights on files that are not its own.
.TP
.B [features]
Hidden features, off unless switched on here or by the environment. They are not settings of the pages
and leave no trace in the interface while they are off.
.RS
.TP
.BR download " = " true | false
Administrators may take the original files of the library (one title, or an album as a ZIP archive).
Every download is written to the change log.
.B DAVID_FEATURE_DOWNLOAD
wins over the file, also when it switches the feature off.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
user = "david"
acl = true
listen = ["0.0.0.0:8484", "[::]:8484"]
mcp = true
groups = ["cdrom"]

[features]
download = true
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/david.toml
This file.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/sysusers.d/david.conf
Creates the account
.BR david .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR david (8),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR setfacl (1)
